cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Conic records: explicit equations for record quartic surfaces with 656, 664, 704, 736, 660 and 640 conics"
authors:
  - name: "Moki & Julio"
version: "1.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24413371"
license: CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - quartic surface
  - K3 surface
  - conics on surfaces
  - real algebraic geometry
  - Neron-Severi lattice
  - Noether-Lefschetz locus
  - Barth-Bauer quartic
  - Goursat surface
  - Dwork pencil
  - certified computation
  - computer algebra
  - Lean 4
abstract: >-
  Degtyarev proved that a smooth quartic surface carries at most 800 conics and described the record configurations
  by lattice data; for most of them no equation was found at our search floor (November 2026), including the real record, a real quartic with 656 real
  conics (configuration (5.14), real bound 656 to 718). This release gives explicit equations, with exact conic counts,
  for six of these surfaces: the real record E w^4 - E q w^2 + q^2 + t p4 = 0 over the cubic field of discriminant -244
  (exactly 656 conics, all real with real points), a member of the W(D4) net with 664 conics over Q(sqrt(-5)), the
  704-conic Barth-Bauer quartic as the member 52 lambda^2 + 36 lambda + 9 = 0 of the W(B4) pencil, the 736-conic quartic
  -x^4 + y^4 + z^4 + 9w^4 + 6xyzw = 0 (a Dwork CM point), the 660-conic member of the Hashimoto pencil, and the 640-conic
  Barth-Bauer quartic (another Dwork CM point). For each surface every conic is written out exactly over a number field,
  the identities are checked in PARI/GP and Singular, the intersection Gram matrix is computed exactly or with
  certified ball arithmetic, and a lattice census on that Gram proves the count is exact and that there are no lines;
  explicit lattice isomorphisms match the 704, 736, 660 and 640 surfaces with Degtyarev's configurations, 664 is
  matched by lattice invariants, and 656 by an explicit match on a numerical Gram with certified invariants. It also maps the conic counts over
  Goursat's octahedral family (eight Noether-Lefschetz curves, completeness argued, an additive rule, the 656 point in the unique all-real
  chamber, the Mukai quartic as a node), shows the family has no hidden symmetry, records an untested quaternion
  (discriminant 6) structure, reports two misprints in the literature (one argued), and independently reproduces six certified
  generic counts of Pichon-Pharabod and Telen. Sealed (pre-registered) tests, all transcripts and the re-check scripts
  are included. The surfaces' existence, lattices and bounds are Degtyarev's; the families are classical or published
  and are credited. Code is MIT-licensed (LICENSE-CODE). Written with AI assistance.
